<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-practice-real-roots" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Practice: Real Roots</title>

  <p>
    Recall that for <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m>, if the characteristic equation has:
    <ul>
      <li><p><alert>Two distinct real roots</alert> <m>r_1 \neq r_2</m>: general solution <m>y = c_1 e^{r_1 x} + c_2 e^{r_2 x}</m></p></li>
      <li><p><alert>One repeated real root</alert> <m>r_1</m>: general solution <m>y = c_1 e^{r_1 x} + c_2 x e^{r_1 x}</m></p></li>
    </ul>
    We justified the distinct roots case on a previous page. Let's justify the repeated root case now.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-repeated-root-justification">
    <title>Why Does the Repeated Root Case Give <m>xe^{r_1 x}</m>?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>r_1</m> is a repeated root of <m>ar^2 + br + c = 0</m>, then
        <m>y_1 = e^{r_1 x}</m> and <m>y_2 = xe^{r_1 x}</m> are two linearly
        independent solutions to <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <title>Justification (can skim)</title>
      <p>
        Since <m>r_1</m> is a repeated root, the characteristic equation factors as
        <m>a(r - r_1)^2 = 0</m>, which means <m>b = -2ar_1</m> and <m>c = ar_1^2</m>.
        We already know <m>y_1 = e^{r_1 x}</m> is a solution. We need to verify
        <m>y_2 = xe^{r_1 x}</m> is also a solution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Computing the derivatives of <m>y_2 = xe^{r_1 x}</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>y_2' \amp= e^{r_1 x} + r_1 xe^{r_1 x} = (1 + r_1 x)e^{r_1 x}</mrow>
          <mrow>y_2'' \amp= r_1 e^{r_1 x} + r_1(1 + r_1 x)e^{r_1 x} = (2r_1 + r_1^2 x)e^{r_1 x}</mrow>
        </md>
        Substituting into <m>ay_2'' + by_2' + cy_2</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>\amp= a(2r_1 + r_1^2 x)e^{r_1 x} + b(1 + r_1 x)e^{r_1 x} + cxe^{r_1 x}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= e^{r_1 x}\left[(2ar_1 + b) + (ar_1^2 + br_1 + c)x\right]</mrow>
        </md>
        Since <m>r_1</m> is a root: <m>ar_1^2 + br_1 + c = 0</m>. And since it is a
        <em>repeated</em> root: <m>2ar_1 + b = 0</m>. So both terms vanish:
        <me>ay_2'' + by_2' + cy_2 = e^{r_1 x}[0 + 0] = 0. \checkmark</me>
        Finally, <m>y_1 = e^{r_1 x}</m> and <m>y_2 = xe^{r_1 x}</m> are not constant
        multiples of each other, so they are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    These first two cases are straightforward enough, but the activity below has some fun twists
    where it may not be immediately obvious how to proceed.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-real-roots-practice">
    <title>Practice: Real Roots</title>

    <task xml:id="task-real-roots-1">
      <title>Solve <m>y'' + 2y' + y = 0</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the general solution to <m>y'' + 2y' + y = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 + 2r + 1 = 0</m>. Try factoring it!
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 + 2r + 1 = 0</m>, which factors as:
          <me>(r + 1)^2 = 0.</me>
          This gives a repeated root <m>r_1 = -1</m>. The general solution is:
          <me>y = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 x e^{-x}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-real-roots-2">
      <title>Solve <m>y'' - y' = 0</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the general solution to <m>y'' - y' = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 - r = 0</m>. Factor out an <m>r</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 - r = 0</m>, which factors as:
          <me>r(r - 1) = 0.</me>
          So <m>r_1 = 0</m> and <m>r_2 = 1</m>. These are two distinct real roots, giving:
          <me>y_1 = e^{0 \cdot x} = 1 \qquad \text{and} \qquad y_2 = e^{x}.</me>
          The general solution is:
          <me>y = c_1 + c_2 e^{x}.</me>
          Notice that <m>r = 0</m> as a root just gives a constant solution: perfectly valid!
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-real-roots-3">
      <title>Solve <m>y'' = 0</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>Find the general solution to <m>y'' = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 = 0</m>. What are the roots?
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 = 0</m>, giving a repeated root
          <m>r_1 = 0</m>. By the repeated root case:
          <me>y_1 = e^{0 \cdot x} = 1 \qquad \text{and} \qquad y_2 = xe^{0 \cdot x} = x.</me>
          The general solution is:
          <me>y = c_1 + c_2 x.</me>
          This makes sense: if the second derivative is zero, the function must be linear!
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The method always applies:</alert> any ODE of the form <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m>
          has a characteristic equation <m>ar^2 + br + c = 0</m>. Even when the ODE looks unusual,
          like <m>y'' - y' = 0</m> or <m>y'' = 0</m>, you can always identify <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>
          and proceed the same way.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Watch for surprises:</alert> a root of <m>r = 0</m> just gives a constant or linear solution,
          which is perfectly valid.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

